WHAT: on __wasi__, give libqalculate's Thread an in-memory message FIFO and run
      each thread body INLINE from sleep_ms(), instead of pthreads.
WHY:  this is the patch the port exists on. wasm32-wasip2 has no threads --
      wasi-libc defines pthread_create as a stub returning ENOTSUP -- so
      upstream's Thread::start() fails and EVERY caller takes its
      `mstruct->setAborted()` error path. Measured on the unpatched library
      under wasmtime 47:

          CALCULATOR->calculate(&m, "6*7", 2000, eo)
            -> rc=0 aborted=1 elapsed=0 ms result="aborted"

      i.e. the Qt GUI would render the word "aborted" for every expression a
      user types, and it would look like a Qt or QPA bug rather than a
      libqalculate one. (pthread_cancel is a second, smaller failure: wasi-sdk
      declares it only under __wasi_cooperative_threads__ with the comment
      "WASI has no cancellation support", so Thread::cancel() does not even
      compile. This patch removes that call site along with the rest.)

      THE SHAPE. Every run() body in libqalculate and in the Qt front end --
      CalculateThread, ViewThread, CommandThread -- is

          while(true) { if(!read(&a)) break; ... }

      a message loop that already stops cleanly when its queue runs dry. So
      draining the queue and returning IS the loop's own exit condition, and
      no body needed touching. write() appends bytes to a std::vector (byte-
      exact with the fwrite() it replaces, because the readers read fixed
      scalar widths back), start() only marks the thread live, and the body
      runs on the caller's stack the next time anything calls sleep_ms().

      sleep_ms() is the hook because every waiter in BOTH projects already
      spins on it right after writing its messages -- Calculator::calculate(),
      Calculator::convertTimeOut(), Calculator::abort(), terminateThreads(),
      and, on the Qt side, QalculateWindow::setResult() and ::executeCommand().
      That is why this port needs no patch to qalculate-qt's threading at all.

      WHAT IS LOST, honestly: the msecs timeout. Upstream enforces it by
      watching the clock from the calling thread while the worker runs; with
      one stack there is nobody to watch, so a runaway expression blocks the
      node rather than returning "timed out". The progress dialog and its
      Cancel button are unreachable for the same reason -- which at least
      means nobody is shown a frozen dialog. The cooperative
      startControl(msecs) ceiling still applies everywhere the caller sets one
      (printing, the CLI-style entry points); it is the GUI's
      calculate(..., 0, ...) path that has none.
UPSTREAM: not-as-is. Upstream would want this as a proper single-threaded
      backend selected by configure (they already carry a _WIN32 arm of the
      same class), not as a third #ifdef inside the POSIX one; and they would
      want the timeout preserved, which needs cooperation from the evaluator
      rather than from the caller.
--- a/libqalculate/util.cc
+++ b/libqalculate/util.cc
@@ -68,6 +68,24 @@ bool eqstr::operator()(const char *s1, const char *s2) const {
 #endif
 
 void sleep_ms(int milliseconds) {
+#ifdef __wasi__
+	/* THE HOOK. Every waiter in libqalculate AND in the Qt front end spins on
+	 * sleep_ms() immediately after handing a Thread its messages:
+	 *
+	 *   Calculator::calculate()   for(i = 0; b_busy && ...) sleep_ms(1);
+	 *   Calculator::convertTimeOut()               likewise
+	 *   Calculator::abort() / terminateThreads()   likewise
+	 *   QalculateWindow::setResult()   for(...) sleep_ms(1); then
+	 *                                  while(b_busy) {processEvents(); sleep_ms(10);}
+	 *   QalculateWindow::executeCommand()          same shape
+	 *
+	 * so pumping here runs the thread body at exactly the point the caller
+	 * expects the other thread to be making progress, with no call-site
+	 * patches at all. The body then clears b_busy before sleep_ms() returns
+	 * and the spin exits on its first iteration. Falls through to the real
+	 * sleep below so that any OTHER use of sleep_ms keeps its meaning. */
+	Thread::wasiPumpAll();
+#endif
 #ifdef _WIN32
 	Sleep(milliseconds);
 #elif _POSIX_C_SOURCE >= 199309L
@@ -1139,6 +1157,105 @@ bool Thread::cancel() {
 	return true;
 }
 
+#elif defined(__wasi__)
+
+/* wasm32-wasip2 has NO threads. wasi-libc defines pthread_create as a stub
+ * returning ENOTSUP, so upstream's Thread::start() fails, every caller takes
+ * its `mstruct->setAborted()` error path, and the Qt GUI displays "aborted"
+ * for EVERY expression. Measured before this patch:
+ *
+ *   CALCULATOR->calculate(&m, "6*7", 2000, eo) -> rc=0 aborted=1 in 0 ms
+ *
+ * The fix keeps the whole Thread interface and changes only where the body
+ * runs: messages go into an in-memory FIFO instead of a pipe, start() is a
+ * no-op that just marks the thread live, and the body runs INLINE on the
+ * caller's stack the next time anyone calls sleep_ms() (see the note there).
+ *
+ * This works because every run() body in both projects -- CalculateThread,
+ * ViewThread, CommandThread -- is literally
+ *
+ *     while(true) { if(!read(&a)) break; ...; }
+ *
+ * i.e. it already stops cleanly when the message queue runs dry. Draining the
+ * queue and returning IS the loop's own termination condition, so no body had
+ * to be touched.
+ *
+ * What is genuinely lost, and it is not nothing: the msecs TIMEOUT. Upstream
+ * enforces it by letting the caller watch the clock while the thread works
+ * and calling abort() if it overruns. With one stack there is nobody to
+ * watch, so a runaway expression blocks the node instead of raising the
+ * "timed out" result. libqalculate's cooperative startControl(msecs) still
+ * applies wherever the caller sets it (printing, the CLI-style entry points);
+ * it is the GUI's calculate(..., 0, ...) path that has no ceiling. The Cancel
+ * button in the progress dialog is unreachable for the same reason -- and so
+ * is the dialog, which is why nobody sees a frozen one.
+ */
+
+static std::vector<Thread*> *wasi_pending = NULL;
+static bool wasi_pumping = false;
+
+Thread::Thread() : running(false), m_pos(0) {}
+
+Thread::~Thread() {
+	cancel();
+}
+
+void Thread::wasiSchedule() {
+	if(!wasi_pending) wasi_pending = new std::vector<Thread*>;
+	for(size_t i = 0; i < wasi_pending->size(); i++) {
+		if((*wasi_pending)[i] == this) return;
+	}
+	wasi_pending->push_back(this);
+}
+
+void Thread::wasiPumpAll() {
+	/* Not reentrant on purpose: a run() body that itself calls sleep_ms()
+	 * (Calculator::abort() does) must not re-enter its own run(). The outer
+	 * loop below still picks up anything queued while it was running. */
+	if(wasi_pumping || !wasi_pending) return;
+	wasi_pumping = true;
+	while(!wasi_pending->empty()) {
+		Thread *thread = wasi_pending->front();
+		wasi_pending->erase(wasi_pending->begin());
+		if(!thread->running) continue;
+		thread->run();
+		/* compact: run() stopped because the FIFO ran dry, so anything left
+		 * is a prefix nobody will ever complete. */
+		if(thread->m_pos > 0) {
+			thread->m_buf.erase(thread->m_buf.begin(), thread->m_buf.begin() + thread->m_pos);
+			thread->m_pos = 0;
+		}
+	}
+	wasi_pumping = false;
+}
+
+void Thread::enableAsynchronousCancel() {
+	/* Nothing to cancel asynchronously, and wasi-libc's pthread_setcanceltype
+	 * is a stub. */
+}
+
+bool Thread::start() {
+	/* No thread is created. `running` is what every caller polls to decide
+	 * whether the worker is alive, and it has to stay true across calls:
+	 * QalculateWindow::executeCommand() reads `if(!commandThread->running)
+	 * command_aborted = true;` after its wait. */
+	running = true;
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool Thread::cancel() {
+	running = false;
+	m_buf.clear();
+	m_pos = 0;
+	if(wasi_pending) {
+		for(size_t i = 0; i < wasi_pending->size();) {
+			if((*wasi_pending)[i] == this) wasi_pending->erase(wasi_pending->begin() + i);
+			else i++;
+		}
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
 #else
 
 Thread::Thread() : running(false), m_pipe_r(NULL), m_pipe_w(NULL) {
